      "selectedNodes": [
        {
          "type": "simulation",
          "id": "bus-seats-model",
          "title": "Hourly seats vs. hourly demand",
          "brief": "First predict: if you double buses per hour from 8 to 16, will seats available per hour double too? Then move the slider and compare. Note the 'extra riders attracted per added bus' slider — if riders grow as fast as capacity, spare seats can vanish even as service improves. A negative 'seats available' means a shortfall (turn-aways).",
          "parameters": [
            {
              "id": "busesPerHour",
              "label": "Buses per hour",
              "unit": "buses",
              "min": 1,
              "max": 30,
              "step": 1,
              "value": 8
            },
            {
              "id": "seatsPerBus",
              "label": "Seats per bus",
              "unit": "seats",
              "min": 20,
              "max": 80,
              "step": 2,
              "value": 40
            },
            {
              "id": "baseRiders",
              "label": "Riders who ride regardless of frequency",
              "unit": "riders",
              "min": 0,
              "max": 600,
              "step": 10,
              "value": 300
            },
            {
              "id": "extraRidersPerBus",
              "label": "Extra riders attracted per added bus",
              "unit": "riders",
              "min": 0,
              "max": 40,
              "step": 1,
              "value": 8
            }
          ],
          "readouts": [
            {
              "id": "capacity",
              "label": "Seats offered per hour",
              "unit": "seats",
              "expr": "busesPerHour * seatsPerBus",
              "precision": 0
            },
            {
              "id": "riders",
              "label": "Riders per hour",
              "unit": "riders",
              "expr": "baseRiders + extraRidersPerBus * busesPerHour",
              "precision": 0
            },
            {
              "id": "available",
              "label": "Seats available per hour",
              "unit": "seats",
              "expr": "busesPerHour * seatsPerBus - baseRiders - extraRidersPerBus * busesPerHour",
              "precision": 0,
              "emphasis": true
            },
            {
              "id": "loadFactor",
              "label": "Load factor",
              "unit": "%",
              "expr": "(baseRiders + extraRidersPerBus * busesPerHour) / (busesPerHour * seatsPerBus) * 100",
              "precision": 1
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "selectedOutsideWords": 98,
      "selectedOutsideText": "Nice — that's a hands-on way to see it. Here's a live model you can drive yourself: change buses per hour (and the other assumptions) and watch seats available per hour update instantly.\n\n\n\nA couple of things worth trying once you've played with it: set *extra riders per added bus* to a high value to see how demand can cancel out a service increase, and then set it to 0 to see the pure capacity effect.\n\nTell me what you find and where the number stops improving — that point is usually where the interesting policy conversation lives.",
      "selectedText": "Nice — that's a hands-on way to see it. Here's a live model you can drive yourself: change buses per hour (and the other assumptions) and watch seats available per hour update instantly.\n\n```openui lifecycle=ephemeral id=bus-frequency-seats\nroot = LearningSurface([model], \"Bus frequency → seats available per hour\", \"Move the sliders; the seats-available number updates as you go.\", \"ephemeral\")\nmodel = Simulation(\"bus-seats-model\", \"Hourly seats vs. hourly demand\", [\n  { id: \"busesPerHour\", label: \"Buses per hour\", unit: \"buses\", min: 1, max: 30, step: 1, value: 8 },\n  { id: \"seatsPerBus\", label: \"Seats per bus\", unit: \"seats\", min: 20, max: 80, step: 2, value: 40 },\n  { id: \"baseRiders\", label: \"Riders who ride regardless of frequency\", unit: \"riders\", min: 0, max: 600, step: 10, value: 300 },\n  { id: \"extraRidersPerBus\", label: \"Extra riders attracted per added bus\", unit: \"riders\", min: 0, max: 40, step: 1, value: 8 }\n], [\n  { id: \"capacity\", label: \"Seats offered per hour\", unit: \"seats\", expr: \"busesPerHour * seatsPerBus\", precision: 0 },\n  { id: \"riders\", label: \"Riders per hour\", unit: \"riders\", expr: \"baseRiders + extraRidersPerBus * busesPerHour\", precision: 0 },\n  { id: \"available\", label: \"Seats available per hour\", unit: \"seats\", expr: \"busesPerHour * seatsPerBus - baseRiders - extraRidersPerBus * busesPerHour\", precision: 0, emphasis: true },\n  { id: \"loadFactor\", label: \"Load factor\", unit: \"%\", expr: \"(baseRiders + extraRidersPerBus * busesPerHour) / (busesPerHour * seatsPerBus) * 100\", precision: 1 }\n], \"ephemeral\", \"First predict: if you double buses per hour from 8 to 16, will seats available per hour double too? Then move the slider and compare. Note the 'extra riders attracted per added bus' slider — if riders grow as fast as capacity, spare seats can vanish even as service improves. A negative 'seats available' means a shortfall (turn-aways).\")\n```\n\nA couple of things worth trying once you've played with it: set *extra riders per added bus* to a high value to see how demand can cancel out a service increase, and then set it to 0 to see the pure capacity effect.\n\nTell me what you find and where the number stops improving — that point is usually where the interesting policy conversation lives.",
      "selectedNativeTools": [],
      "nativeOnly": false,
      "verdict": true,
      "note": "A valid Simulation exposes buses per hour and seats per bus. At the authored defaults, changing 8 buses/hour to 16 changes offered capacity from 320 to 640 and spare seats from −44 to 212 using the declared demand model. The brief invites a prediction; it never asks the learner to submit one for assessment. The extra demand model and surrounding prose make this more elaborate than the learner requested.",
